We now learned both filters and nets. As it is mentioned in earlier, both
concepts are made to generalize sequences. Then one might question about the
correspondence between filters and nets. Indeed, one can canonically construct a
filter from a net, and vice versa, preserving their convergence properties
seamlessly.

#label(defin:eventuality-filter)
useenv defin [Eventuality filters and canonical nets] {
    Let $X$ be a nonempty set.
    #enum ((\roman*)) {
        \item If $(x_\alpha)_{\alpha\in A}$ is a net in $X$, the collection of
        all sets $E\subset X$ such that $(x_\alpha)_{\alpha\in A}$ is eventually
        in $E$ forms a filter. We call this the {\itshape eventuality
        filter}\index{net!eventuality filter} of the net
        $(x_\alpha)_{\alpha\in A}$, and denote it by $\Fc(x_\alpha)$.
        \item If $\Fc$ is a filter in $X$, define a set
        $D_{\Fc} = \{(x, F) : F\in\Fc, x\in F\}$. Equip $D_{\Fc}$ with a
        preorder $<=$ defined by $(x, F) <= (y, G)$ if $G\subset F$. Then
        $D_{\Fc}$ is a directed set. The net $N_{\Fc}$ defined by
        $N_{\Fc}(x, F) = x$ from $D_{\Fc}$ to $X$ is called the {\itshape
        canonical net}\index{filter!canonical net} of the filter $\Fc$.
    }
}

#label(exec:eventuality-filter)
useenv exec {
    Prove that $\Fc(x_\alpha)$ is indeed a filter in $X$, and that $D_{\Fc}$ is
    a directed set.
}

#label(thm:net-filter-convergence)
useenv thm {
    Let $X$ be a topological space and $x\in X$.
    #enum ((\alph*)) {
        \item A net $(x_\alpha)_{\alpha\in A}$ converges to $x$ if and only if
        its eventuality filter $\Fc(x_\alpha)$ converges to $x$.
        \item A filter $\Fc$ converges to $x$ if and only if its canonical net
        $N_{\Fc}$ converges to $x$.
        \item A point $x$ is a cluster point of $(x_\alpha)_{\alpha\in A}$ if
        and only if $x\in\bigcap_{E\in\Fc(x_\alpha)} \overline{E}$.
    }
    \proof
    (a) By definition, a net $(x_\alpha)_{\alpha\in A}$ converges to $x$ if and
    only if it is eventually in every open neighborhood $U$ of $x$. This is
    exactly equivalent to saying that every open neighborhood $U$ of $x$ belongs
    to $\Fc(x_\alpha)$, which means $\Fc(x_\alpha) -> x$.

    (b) Suppose $\Fc -> x$. For any open neighborhood $U$ of $x$, $U\in\Fc$.
    Take any $y\in U$. Then $(y, U)\in D_{\Fc}$. For any $(z, G) >= (y, U)$, we
    have $G\subset U$, hence $z\in G\subset U$. This implies the net $N_{\Fc}$
    is eventually in $U$, so $N_{\Fc} -> x$.

    Conversely, if $N_{\Fc} -> x$, then for any open neighborhood $U$ of $x$,
    there exists $(y, F)\in D_{\Fc}$ such that for all $(z, G) >= (y, F)$,
    $z\in U$. In particular, for any $z\in F$, we have $(z, F) >= (y, F)$,
    meaning $z\in U$. Thus $F\subset U$. Since $F\in\Fc$ and $\Fc$ is a filter,
    $U\in\Fc$. Therefore, $\Fc -> x$.

    (c) Suppose $x$ is a cluster point of $(x_\alpha)_{\alpha\in A}$. This means
    for any $U\in\Nc_x$ and any $\alpha\in A$, there is $\gamma >= \alpha$ such
    that $x_\gamma\in U$. By the definition of $\Fc(x_\alpha)$, every
    $E\in\Fc(x_\alpha)$ contains a tail set
    $T_\alpha = \{x_\gamma : \gamma >= \alpha\}$ for some $\alpha$. Thus
    $U\cap E\supset U\cap T_\alpha\neq\emptyset$. Since $U$ is arbitrary,
    $x\in\overline{E}$, hence $x\in\bigcap_{E\in\Fc(x_\alpha)} \overline{E}$.

    Conversely, if $x\in\bigcap_{E\in\Fc(x_\alpha)} \overline{E}$, then for any
    $U\in\Nc_x$ and $\alpha\in A$, since the tail $T_\alpha\in\Fc(x_\alpha)$, we
    have $x\in\overline{T_\alpha}$, which implies $U\cap T_\alpha\neq\emptyset$.
    This means there is $\gamma >= \alpha$ with $x_\gamma\in U$. Thus $x$ is a
    cluster point. \proved
}

Not only the convergence, but the maximality concepts (universal nets and
ultrafilters) also perfectly correspond to each other.

#label(thm:ultra-universal)
useenv thm {
    Let $X$ be a set.
    #enum ((\alph*)) {
        \item If $(x_\alpha)_{\alpha\in A}$ is a universal net in $X$, then
        $\Fc(x_\alpha)$ is an ultrafilter in $X$.
        \item If $\Uc$ is an ultrafilter in $X$, then $N_{\Uc}$ is a universal
        net in $X$.
    }
    \proof
    (a) Suppose $(x_\alpha)_{\alpha\in A}$ is a universal net. To show
    $\Fc(x_\alpha)$ is an ultrafilter, by \Cref{prop:equiv-ultrafilters}, it
    suffices to show that for any $E\subset X$, either $E$ or $X-!E$ belongs to
    $\Fc(x_\alpha)$. Since the net is universal, it is eventually in $E$ or
    eventually in $X-!E$ (\Cref{prop:equiv-universal-net}). By definition, this
    means either $E\in\Fc(x_\alpha)$ or $X-!E\in\Fc(x_\alpha)$, proving the
    claim.

    (b) Suppose $\Uc$ is an ultrafilter. To show $N_{\Uc}$ is a universal net,
    we use \Cref{prop:equiv-universal-net}. Let $E\subset X$. Since $\Uc$ is an
    ultrafilter, either $E\in\Uc$ or $X-!E\in\Uc$. Assume $E\in\Uc$. Fix any
    $y\in E$. For any $(z, G) >= (y, E)$, we have $G\subset E$, so
    $z\in G\subset E$. This shows that $N_{\Uc}$ is eventually in $E$. By the
    same argument, if $X-!E\in\Uc$, $N_{\Uc}$ is eventually in $X-!E$. Thus
    $N_{\Uc}$ is a universal net. \proved
}
